/// **This asserted `.inline` and no stream until the column existed**, on the
/// reasoning that a sidebar has nothing to float. True of a window, false of the
/// viewport: a sidebar has width to spare, so it leaves the tab rather than stays.
///
/// **Selecting Live must not move it either, and that half is a bug fix.** Two
/// mount points for one `RealityView` is what `ReachyScene/AGENTS.md` forbids, and
/// instrumenting the real screen showed exactly that: a scene made and torn down
/// 4 ms later, `containerAlreadyParented=true` proving the entity was reparented
/// between scenes, and more teardowns than mounts — so `onDisappear` cannot even
/// be used to sequence the two. A sidebar therefore has **one** host, always.
///
/// `rest` is ignored for the same reason it always was: a column has neither corner
/// nor edge, so rotating an iPad must not land the reader somewhere decided by a
/// gesture made in the other orientation.
@Test("a sidebar has one host, whatever the tab and the window were doing")
func regularWidthUsesTheColumn() {
    let rests: [FloatingViewportModel.Placement] = [
        .floating(.topLeading),
        .docked(.leading, y: 200),
        .docked(.trailing, y: 600),
    ]
    for rest in rests {
        for live in [false, true] {
            let model = model(rest, hasTabBar: false, live: live)
            #expect(model.placement == .column)
            #expect(model.isStreaming)
            #expect(!model.isInline)
            #expect(!model.isWindowed)
        }
    }
}

